---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro'
import EarlyAccessForm from '../components/EarlyAccessForm.astro'

const title = 'About | Klaroly'
const description =
  'Klaroly is built by Sunday South, a UK studio, alongside professional wedding makeup artists. Here is why we are building it.'
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description}>
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-4xl px-5 sm:px-8">
    <section class="pt-16 pb-2 sm:pt-24">
      <h1 class="text-4xl leading-[1.1] font-semibold tracking-tight sm:text-5xl">
        Why we are building this
      </h1>
      <p
        class="mt-6 max-w-xl text-lg leading-relaxed text-[var(--color-muted)] dark:text-[var(--color-muted-dark)]"
      >
        Klaroly started with a professional makeup artist complaining about her
        admin to her brother-in-law.
      </p>
    </section>

    <div class="prose max-w-2xl">
      <p>
        The artist is <a
          href="https://www.instagram.com/jessicabmakeupartist/"
          rel="noopener">Jess</a
        >. The brother-in-law is <a href="https://www.jackbarham.com" rel="noopener"
          >Jack</a
        >, who builds software for a living. The complaint was not about the
        work, because the work is the good bit. It was about the twelve places a
        booking lives before it becomes a wedding: the Instagram message, the
        email, the note in the phone, the calendar entry, the contract template
        that never quite gets updated, the invoice, the reminder that did not get
        sent.
      </p>
      <p>
        There is software for this. Most of it was built for salons, where the
        client comes to you and books a forty-minute slot. A wedding is a
        different shape entirely. It is booked eighteen months out, it involves
        five people, it happens somewhere you have to drive to at five in the
        morning, and the money arrives in two pieces months apart. Bending salon
        software around that is possible. It is just not much fun.
      </p>
      <p>So we decided to build something for the actual shape of the job.</p>

      <h2>Who is behind it</h2>
      <p>
        Klaroly is made by <strong>Sunday South</strong>, a small UK studio that has
        been building web and mobile products for other people for fifteen years.
        This one is ours.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Not funded by anybody.</strong> No investors, no growth targets, no
        pressure to add features nobody asked for. It is paid for by the people who
        use it, which keeps the incentives simple.
      </p>

      <h2>Are you a hair or makeup artist?</h2>
      <p>
        If you handle weddings or on-site bookings, we want to hear from you.
        Help us finish this off, and shape the features you want most. That is
        not a polite line. The order things get built in is being set by the
        artists we are talking to.
      </p>
    </div>

    <EarlyAccessForm id="about" class="mt-2" />
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
